{"record_id":"R0082","title":"LLHR: Low Latency and High Reliability CNN Distributed Inference for Resource-Constrained UAV Swarms","authors":["Marwan Dhuheir","Aiman Erbad","Sinan Sabeeh"],"year":2023,"venue":"arXiv:2305.15858v1 [cs.DC] (arXiv identity page lists Journal reference: 2023 IEEE Wireless Communications and Networking Conference (WCNC), pp. 1-6; IEEE publisher PDF not locally retained and version equivalence not inferred)","doi":"10.1109/WCNC55385.2023.10118908","arxiv_doi":"10.48550/arXiv.2305.15858","identity_source":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.15858","source_version":"arXiv:2305.15858v1 [cs.DC] 25 May 2023 (no printed page numbers; physical pages 1-6 only)","source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0082.pdf","physical_pdf_pages":6,"access":"VERIFIED","physical_page":1,"section":"I","excerpt":"All the data processing load for different requests can be handled locally within the swarm without resorting to remote servers."}
{"record_id":"R0082","key":"topology","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"Decentralized wireless ad hoc network of U UAVs flying at the same altitude with 2D coordinates (x_i, y_i) over a monitored area divided into v x q cells (480m x 480m with 144 cells of 40m x 40m and radius R = 20m in Sec. IV); direct UAV-to-UAV Line of Sight (LoS) links are assumed (NLoS ignored), with no master UAV or remote-server offloading during inference.","source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0082.pdf","physical_page":2,"section":"II","excerpt":"we assume that a group of UAVs, denoted by U, is capable to form a wireless ad hoc network."}
{"record_id":"R0082","key":"workload","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"Real-time ground-surveillance image classification requests (RQ_i per UAV per time frame T, total RQ = sum RQ_i; varied up to R = 20 in Figs. 3 and 5) executed on two pre-trained CNNs: 5-layer LeNet (2 convolutional and 3 fully connected layers, 32x32x3 RGB input) and 8-layer AlexNet (5 convolutional and 3 fully connected layers, 227x227x3 RGB input).","source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0082.pdf","physical_page":5,"section":"IV","excerpt":"In the experiments, we used two different CNNs, a small CNN, namely LeNet (2 convolutional layers and 3 fully connected layers)"}
{"record_id":"R0082","key":"partition","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"Per-layer sequential CNN distribution across L layers (j = 1..L); each UAV holds a copy of the trained CNN and executes assigned layers via binary decision variable delta_{r,i,j} in {0,1} so each layer of request r is computed by exactly one UAV (Eq. 11c) under memory m_bar_i (Eq. 11a) and compute c_bar_i (Eq. 11b) limits, delegating subtasks when capacity is insufficient; optimization runs periodically.","source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0082.pdf","physical_page":4,"section":"III-C","excerpt":"We emphasize that we adopted the method of per-layer distribution as it is suitable for systems with a limited number of UAVs."}
{"record_id":"R0082","key":"solver","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"Sequential three-subproblem decomposition: P1 (transmit power minimization, called a convex MILP in Eq. 6; text notes combining traditional convex optimization algorithms with exhaustive search), P2 (UAV grid position optimization with collision-avoidance d_{i,k} >= 2R, called a QCQP in Eq. 9), and P3 (layer allocation minimizing latency, called an ILP in Eq. 11 despite bilinear term delta_{r,i,j}*delta_{r,k,j+1}). Specific solver software, linearization method, and runtime are not reported.","source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0082.pdf","physical_page":4,"section":"III-A, III-B, III-C","excerpt":"a combination of traditional convex optimization algorithms with simple algorithms like exhaustive search can be used to obtain the optimal solution"}
{"record_id":"R0082","key":"evaluation","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"Simulation-only evaluation (no physical testbed or named simulator) on a 480m x 480m grid (144 cells of 40m x 40m, R = 20m) using three UAV device profiles based on Raspberry Pi B+ / 3B+ (1.4 GHz 64-bit quad-core, 1 GB RAM; e_i = 560, 512, 256 multiplications/s in Sec. IV text, labeled m = 560, 512, 256 in Fig. 3), sigma^2 = -170 dBm, h_0 = 10^-5, P_max = 120 mW (swept 0.05-0.2 W in Fig. 2), tau = 10^-4 (unit omitted). Evaluates average latency (ms/s) and average minimum power (mW) across UAV counts (5, 7, 8, 9, 10), bandwidths (10, 20 MHz; swept up to ~37 MHz in Fig. 4), and requests (R = 1..20), comparing LLHR against Heuristic Solution (static path) and Random Selection Baseline (random movement) in Fig. 5.","source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0082.pdf","physical_page":5,"section":"IV","excerpt":"We adopted an area of 480m x 480m to be covered by the UAV swarm, in which we have 144 equal size cells with 40m width and 40m length with radius R of 20m."}
{"record_id":"R0082","key":"resilience","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"Reliability is modeled strictly as a lower-bound Shannon rate threshold P_i >= P_i^{th} to transmit packet K_j within duration tau over LoS links (Eqs. 5-7), plus collision avoidance d_{i,k} >= 2R (Eq. 8d) and subtask delegation on resource exhaustion (Sec. II, III-C). No UAV node failures, crash recovery, packet-loss/outage handling, NLoS impairment, or fault-injection experiments are modeled or evaluated; Sec. V defers interference and NLoS modes to future work.","source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0082.pdf","physical_page":3,"section":"II, III-A, III-B","excerpt":"The driven threshold is set to assure that the data transmission is reliable among the connected UAVs."}
{"record_id":"R0082","key":"D01","status":"PARTIAL","finding":"System model defines CNN compute/memory loads (Eqs. 1-3), LoS channel/rate (Eqs. 4-5), and subproblems P1-P3 (Eqs. 6-14), but contains formulation inconsistencies: P1 is called an MILP despite continuous power variables only, P3 is called an ILP despite bilinear product delta_{r,i,j}*delta_{r,k,j+1} in Eq. 11, Eq. 3 prints l instead of b, and Eq. 12 indexes rho_{i,k} instead of rho_{s,k}.","source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0082.pdf","physical_page":4,"section":"II, III-A, III-B, III-C","excerpt":"The objective function minimizes the latency of finding the final classification prediction of the captured image is shown in equation (11)."}
{"record_id":"R0082","key":"D02","status":"PARTIAL","finding":"Core modeling assumptions are stated (uniform altitude 2D coordinates in Sec. II, LoS-only links ignoring NLoS in Sec. II-B, full CNN copy on each UAV in Sec. II-A, circular cell and 2R separation in Sec. III-B), but key assumptions on UAV kinematics/speed, flight vs. RF energy, interference modeling, and the unit of tau = 10^-4 (Sec. IV) are omitted.","source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0082.pdf","physical_page":3,"section":"II, II-B, IV","excerpt":"we assume that the transmission of data between UAVs meets the Line of Sight (LoS) model and ignore the Non LoS (NLOS) in this model."}
{"record_id":"R0082","key":"D03","status":"NO","finding":"Reproducible configuration is absent: Sec. IV gives grid size, sigma^2, h_0, P_max, tau (without unit), and Raspberry Pi B+ profiles, but omits simulator/solver names and versions, layer-wise K_j/m_j/c_j values, m_bar_i/c_bar_i capacities, trial counts/seeds, static path definition for the Heuristic baseline, and has conflicting semantics for 560/512/256 (multiplications/s e_i in Sec. IV text vs. m and memory capacity in Fig. 3). Checked Sections III and IV.","source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0082.pdf","physical_page":5,"section":"IV (checked III, IV)","excerpt":"Particularly, all types are characterized by a 1.4 GHz 64-bit quad-core processor and 1 GB RAM"}
{"record_id":"R0082","key":"D04","status":"PARTIAL","finding":"Compares LLHR in Fig. 5 (Sec. IV, p. 6) against two internal baselines: Heuristic Solution (same setup with static predefined UAV path) and Random Selection Baseline (random UAV movement). However, no external published baseline is benchmarked, Fig. 5 omits the CNN model, UAV count, bandwidth, and device profile used, and no baseline power comparison is shown.","source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0082.pdf","physical_page":6,"section":"IV","excerpt":"Figure 5 compares our LLHR approach with two other approaches, one is a heuristic solution, and the other is a random selection baseline approach as we vary the number of requests."}
{"record_id":"R0082","key":"D05","status":"PARTIAL","finding":"Average latency (ms/s, Figs. 2, 3, 5) and average minimum transmit power (mW, Fig. 4) align with latency and power objectives, but transmission reliability (outage rate, packet delivery ratio, deadline miss rate) and interference are never directly measured or reported despite central reliability and interference-mitigation claims. Checked Section IV and Figs. 2-5.","source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0082.pdf","physical_page":5,"section":"IV","excerpt":"Figure 2 shows the results of latency in case of changing the maximum power of each UAV under a different number of UAVs and different allocated bandwidth to the UAVs"}
{"record_id":"R0082","key":"D06","status":"PARTIAL","finding":"Parameter sensitivity sweeps cover maximum power (Fig. 2), UAV count (Figs. 2, 4), bandwidth (Figs. 2, 4), request count (Figs. 3, 5), CNN model (Figs. 3, 4), and device capacity profile (Fig. 3), but zero statistical variability metrics (standard deviation, confidence intervals, error bars, or run counts) are reported anywhere in Section IV or Figs. 2-5.","source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0082.pdf","physical_page":5,"section":"IV","excerpt":"In the same experiment, we studied the case of different bandwidth allocations between the connected UAVs, namely 10 MHz and 20 MHz"}
{"record_id":"R0082","key":"D07","status":"NO","finding":"Channel model assumes pure free-space LoS path gain (Eq. 4) and ignores NLoS, fading, and co-channel interference (Eq. 5 includes only thermal noise sigma^2); mobility is reduced to fixed-altitude 2D grid coordinates (x_i, y_i) without flight dynamics, speed limits, propulsion energy, or link-disconnection modeling. Checked Sections II-B, III-B, IV, and V.","source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0082.pdf","physical_page":6,"section":"V (checked II-B, III-B, IV, V)","excerpt":"In our future work, we plan to study the effect of interference on transmission latency when controlling channel communication under different modes like a line of sight and a non-line of sight."}
{"record_id":"R0082","key":"D08","status":"NO","finding":"Claims in Abstract, Sec. I, and Sec. V overreach the evidence by asserting guaranteed reliability, interference mitigation, and optimal trajectory planning, whereas interference is absent from Eq. 5 and deferred to future work (Sec. V), reliability is never empirically evaluated, and no exact numerical improvements are printed. Checked Abstract, Sections I, IV, and V.","source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0082.pdf","physical_page":6,"section":"V (checked Abstract, I, IV, V)","excerpt":"while guaranteeing the reliability and finding the best UAV path to mitigate interference."}
{"record_id":"R0082","key":"D09","status":"NO","finding":"No source code, simulation scripts, datasets, or trained model artifacts are linked or provided anywhere in the paper. Checked Abstract, Sections I-V, Acknowledgment, and References (physical pages 1-6).","source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0082.pdf","physical_page":6,"section":"Acknowledgment (checked I-V, Acknowledgment, References)","excerpt":"The findings achieved herein are solely the responsibility of the authors."}
{"record_id":"R0082","key":"D10","status":"PARTIAL","finding":"Funding is declared in Acknowledgment (Qatar National Research Fund NPRP grant # NPRP13S-0205-200265) and Sec. V notes future work on interference and LoS/NLoS modes, but there is no formal limitations section, conflict-of-interest statement, or data-availability declaration; the arXiv v1 identity page also notes substantial text overlap with arXiv:2212.11201. Checked Section V, Acknowledgment, and References.","source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0082.pdf","physical_page":6,"section":"Acknowledgment, V","excerpt":"This work was made possible by NPRP grant # NPRP13S-0205-200265 from the Qatar National Research Fund"}
{"record_id":"R0082","key":"outcomes","status":"PLOT_ONLY","entries":[],"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0082.pdf","locators":[{"figure":"Fig. 2","physical_page":5,"section":"IV","metric":"Average Latency","unit":"ms","direction":"lower is better","conditions":"Maximum Power (W) varied; UAVs = 5 and 7; BW = 10 MHz and 20 MHz; CNN model and request count not stated in caption/text","baselines":[],"status":"PLOT_ONLY"},{"figure":"Fig. 3(a)-(b)","physical_page":5,"section":"IV","metric":"Average Latency / Average Total Latency","unit":"s","direction":"lower is better","conditions":"Number of Requests (R) varied up to 20; 5-Layers LeNet (a) and 8-Layers AlexNet (b); three Raspberry Pi 3B+ device profiles labeled m = 560, 512, 256","baselines":[],"status":"PLOT_ONLY"},{"figure":"Fig. 4(a)-(b)","physical_page":6,"section":"IV","metric":"Average Minimum Power","unit":"mW","direction":"lower is better","conditions":"Available Bandwidth to the UAV (MHz) varied; 5-Layers LeNet with UAVs = 5 and 9 (a); 8-Layers AlexNet with UAVs = 8 and 10 (b)","baselines":[],"status":"PLOT_ONLY"},{"figure":"Fig. 5","physical_page":6,"section":"IV","metric":"Average Latency","unit":"s","direction":"lower is better","conditions":"Number of Requests (R) varied up to 20; CNN model, UAV count, bandwidth, and device profile not stated in caption/text","baselines":["Heuristic Solution","Random Selection Baseline solution"],"status":"PLOT_ONLY"}],"note":"No exact numerical outcome values or percentage gains are printed in text or tables anywhere in physical pages 1-6; all quantitative evaluation results appear exclusively as unlabelled line plots in Figs. 2-5.","physical_page":6,"section":"IV","excerpt":"It is clear that our LLHR approach is outperforming the other approaches."}
